{
  "requirement": "After one valid judgment, the harness MUST write the subject text, submitted\ndetermination and rationale, and recorded inputs using the existing evidence\nschema. {cite}`requirements.ci.schema`\nBy default, it MUST write the subject's evidence file. With `--stdout`, it MUST\nemit a single evidence JSON object followed by a newline to stdout and MUST NOT\ncreate or change evidence files or directories. All three outcomes MUST be\nwritten to the selected destination as completed assessments; their validation\nstatus MUST follow {cite}`requirements.ci.determination`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the sources satisfy `requirements.authoring.persistence`. I found no gaps and no FIXME markers.\n\n**Implementation, each obligation:**\n- **Record content.**\n  - `assess` (`src/authoring/mod.rs`) builds the evidence object only after one validated `submit_judgment`.\n  - The object is `{<requirement|example>: subject_text, determination: judgment.to_value(), rationale, inputs: recorded_inputs()}`, which is the existing schema.\n  - `Judgment::to_value` (`src/judgment.rs`) serialises `true`, `false` and `{\"FIXME\": [...]}`, so all three outcomes are written as completed assessments.\n- **Default file destination.** In `configured_assessment`, `EvidenceOutput::File` creates `evidence/` and writes `evidence/<subject>.json` with a trailing newline.\n- **`--stdout`.**\n  - `EvidenceOutput::Stdout` writes one pretty-printed JSON object plus `\\n` to stdout and flushes.\n  - It creates no `evidence/` file or directory.\n  - Usage state goes to the state directory, which `author_evidence_with_output` requires to be outside the project.\n- **Batch writes.** `write_record` (`src/authoring/batch.rs`) writes the same record through a temp file and an atomic rename. `PendingAssessment::persisted` then records `evidence_written` and the \"completed\" outcome.\n- **Validation status.** That the status follows `requirements.ci.determination` is enforced by the validator. Persistence only has to write `false` and `FIXME` as completed assessments, and it does.\n- **Marking.** The implementing functions carry `{impl}` markers. I found no other persistence code left unmarked, and no unspecified behaviour beyond what the batch and usage requirements cover.\n\n**Tests:**\n- `stdout_emits_only_evidence_json_without_changing_project_files` covers all three outcomes with `--stdout`, plus the verbose variants.\n  - It checks that stdout is a single JSON object ending in `\\n`.\n  - It checks the subject text, determination, rationale and the requirements/definition/outline inputs.\n  - It checks that an existing evidence file is byte-identical, and that a missing `evidence/` directory is not created.\n- `false_is_a_completed_assessment` and `verbose_single_author_reports_only_false_rationale_without_source_inputs` cover default file writes for `false` and `true`. They check that outline inputs store only the selector and hash, never source text, and that usage reports \"completed\".\n- `terminal_progress_distinguishes_judgments_from_authoring_errors` (batch) checks the written `false` and `FIXME` evidence files.\n- The denied-read batch tests check that successful assessments write evidence and the denied ones write none.\n- `explicit_untracked_reads_abort_without_another_request_or_evidence_write` checks that no judgment means the existing file is untouched.\n- `failed_evidence_write_keeps_the_accepted_judgment_in_usage` covers a `FIXME` judgment whose write fails.\n- `live_bedrock_authoring` checks stdout evidence against a real provider.\n\n**Minor observation, not a gap:** the single-subject file path uses a plain `fs::write` while the batch path writes atomically. The requirement does not demand atomicity, so both comply.",
